# PART 3 — COMPLETE 2.5D GRAPHICS OVERHAUL

## 3.1 Environment and level remodeling

Substantially improve the visual quality of the playable world.

Replace basic, empty, repetitive, or placeholder-looking scenery with a cohesive environment that matches the supplied references.

Improve:

- Level geometry and environmental structure.
- Platforms, pathways, terrain, rooms, buildings, and architecture.
- Foreground elements and background scenery.
- Environmental props, decoration, and visual landmarks.
- Surface detail and material variation.
- Depth separation and spatial composition.
- Environmental lighting and atmospheric effects.
- Scene transitions and consistency between different areas.

Use actual meshes, sprites, textures, materials, shaders, or procedural assets appropriate to the existing engine.

Create convincing depth between the foreground, gameplay plane, and background. Use parallax layers when they improve the intended 2.5D presentation.

Avoid flat, empty environments and generic geometric placeholders unless the reference style specifically calls for them.

Maintain gameplay readability. Important platforms, enemies, hazards, and interactable objects must remain distinguishable from decorative scenery.

## 3.2 Character and enemy remodeling

Improve the player's appearance and the presentation of all important characters, enemies, and interactive objects.

Depending on the current implementation, use appropriately detailed 3D models, sprites, skeletal animation, procedural geometry, or a suitable combination.

Improve:

- Silhouette and proportions.
- Textures and materials.
- Surface detail and visual identity.
- Lighting, shadows, and highlights.
- Animation quality and transitions.
- Visual consistency with the surrounding world.

Do not replace detailed character assets with simple primitives or emoji.

Ensure that character models remain correctly aligned with their gameplay positions. Keep visual meshes and collision geometry coordinated without unnecessarily changing gameplay hitboxes.

## 3.3 Materials, textures, and rendering

Use a deliberate material system rather than relying on generic, untextured geometry.

Where supported, implement:

- High-quality textures and correct UV mapping.
- Material roughness and metallic properties.
- Normal maps or other suitable surface-detail techniques.
- Custom shaders and procedural materials.
- Correct transparency and depth handling.
- Reflections and highlights when appropriate.
- Consistent texture resolution and asset scaling.

Improve the rendering pipeline if the current one is limiting visual quality.

If the game uses Three.js or another browser-based 3D framework, inspect the existing renderer configuration, scene graph, camera, lighting, shadow setup, materials, textures, and post-processing before deciding which components to replace.

Retain the current engine unless a genuine technical reason requires otherwise.

## 3.4 Lighting and atmosphere

Rebuild the lighting design to establish depth, mood, and visual coherence.

Implement appropriate combinations of:

- Directional, ambient, and local lighting.
- Cast shadows and contact shadows.
- Reflections and highlights.
- Fog, haze, and environmental atmosphere.
- Bloom and restrained post-processing.
- Color grading and contrast control.
- Environmental and emissive lighting effects.

Lighting should improve material definition and depth rather than obscure gameplay.

Use expensive effects only where they provide meaningful improvements.

